1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,472
This is a GPT that knows nothing.

2
00:00:02,751 --> 00:00:04,623
Not a word of English.

3
00:00:04,903 --> 00:00:07,903
Not even that letters exist in a particular order.

4
00:00:08,184 --> 00:00:11,712
Step one: pure gibberish, random characters.

5
00:00:11,992 --> 00:00:14,968
Now watch what happens as the loss number drops.

6
00:00:15,248 --> 00:00:17,408
Letters start clumping into words.

7
00:00:17,688 --> 00:00:19,944
Words start taking the shape of lines.

8
00:00:20,224 --> 00:00:23,560
Character names with colons appear, then speeches.

9
00:00:23,840 --> 00:00:25,712
It never read a grammar book.

10
00:00:25,992 --> 00:00:29,760
It only ever practised one thing: guessing the next character.

11
00:00:30,040 --> 00:00:35,272
I'll show you the roughly seventy lines of PyTorch behind it, and then we'll train one live.

12
00:00:36,072 --> 00:00:41,568
Before anything else: I built this with Claude Code, and the narration is AI-generated.

13
00:00:41,848 --> 00:00:46,120
The code and the training you'll see are real, and run on my own CPU.

14
00:00:46,400 --> 00:00:49,664
Why build a tiny GPT when you can call a big one?

15
00:00:49,944 --> 00:00:55,968
Because the big ones can feel like magic, and a magic trick stops being scary once you've watched it assembled.

16
00:00:56,248 --> 00:01:10,696
The model in this app has the same ingredients as the systems behind chatbots: token embeddings, positional embeddings, stacked transformer blocks with self-attention, and a final layer that outputs a probability for every possible next token.

17
00:01:10,976 --> 00:01:12,848
The difference is scale.

18
00:01:13,128 --> 00:01:19,416
At the default settings this one has eight hundred and eighteen thousand, one hundred and seventy-six parameters.

19
00:01:19,696 --> 00:01:21,568
That is under a million.

20
00:01:21,848 --> 00:01:23,720
It is also character-level.

21
00:01:24,000 --> 00:01:29,496
The vocabulary is just the sixty-five different characters that appear in the training text.

22
00:01:29,776 --> 00:01:32,008
No words, no tokenizer.

23
00:01:32,288 --> 00:01:36,200
That makes it simple, and it makes the learning stages easy to see.

24
00:01:37,000 --> 00:01:39,592
Everything lives in model.py.

25
00:01:39,872 --> 00:01:43,400
Start with the block, the repeating unit of a transformer.

26
00:01:43,680 --> 00:01:45,552
Two things to notice.

27
00:01:45,832 --> 00:01:47,704
First, the mask.

28
00:01:47,984 --> 00:01:53,192
It's an upper-triangular matrix that blocks each position from looking at later positions.

29
00:01:53,472 --> 00:01:59,976
That is what makes the model causal: when it predicts the next character, it can only use the characters before it.

30
00:02:00,256 --> 00:02:02,608
Without that, training would be cheating.

31
00:02:02,888 --> 00:02:04,856
Second, the plus signs.

32
00:02:05,136 --> 00:02:11,184
The block adds its output to its input, a residual connection, which helps deep stacks train.

33
00:02:11,464 --> 00:02:13,336
Now the full model.

34
00:02:13,616 --> 00:02:24,944
Characters become vectors, the position of each character is added, the stack of blocks mixes information, and the head turns the result back into one score per character in the vocabulary.

35
00:02:25,224 --> 00:02:30,288
The loss is cross-entropy: how surprised was the model by the true next character?

36
00:02:30,568 --> 00:02:33,664
The training data is sliced in a very simple way.

37
00:02:33,944 --> 00:02:40,808
Pick random starting points, take a window as the input, and take the same window shifted by one as the answer.

38
00:02:41,088 --> 00:02:47,712
That is the whole supervision signal: every position is a tiny quiz where the answer is the next character.

39
00:02:47,992 --> 00:02:49,864
And here is how it writes.

40
00:02:50,144 --> 00:02:59,551
Take the scores for the last position, divide by temperature, optionally keep only the top k, turn them into probabilities, and sample one character.

41
00:02:59,831 --> 00:03:01,704
Append it and repeat.

42
00:03:01,983 --> 00:03:08,727
Temperature is the creativity knob: low means safe and repetitive, high means adventurous and messy.

43
00:03:09,007 --> 00:03:19,423
The training loop in the app is the standard four lines: get a batch, compute the loss, zero the gradients, backward, step, using the AdamW optimiser.

44
00:03:19,703 --> 00:03:27,767
The sidebar lets you change layers from one to six, the embedding size, the context length, the number of steps and the learning rate.

45
00:03:28,567 --> 00:03:33,463
The sidebar: training text is Shakespeare, or you can upload your own text file.

46
00:03:33,743 --> 00:03:39,671
Model settings: four layers, embedding size one hundred and twenty-eight, context sixty-four.

47
00:03:39,951 --> 00:03:44,727
Training: six hundred steps, learning rate three e minus three.

48
00:03:45,007 --> 00:03:46,879
I press Train from scratch.

49
00:03:47,159 --> 00:03:54,383
First it reports the parameter count, vocabulary and the size of the text: over a million characters of Shakespeare.

50
00:03:54,663 --> 00:03:58,863
The progress bar shows the step, the loss, and steps per second.

51
00:03:59,143 --> 00:04:10,279
In this recording the loss started at four point three six, which is roughly what you'd expect from guessing among sixty-five characters at random, and had fallen to about two point three by step one hundred.

52
00:04:10,559 --> 00:04:13,463
It ran at about four point four steps per second.

53
00:04:13,743 --> 00:04:18,591
Now the part I love: the section called "What the model writes as it learns".

54
00:04:18,871 --> 00:04:20,743
Step one is noise.

55
00:04:21,023 --> 00:04:25,967
A few hundred steps in, there are real-looking words and line breaks and speaker names.

56
00:04:26,247 --> 00:04:30,183
It isn't coherent, but the shape is unmistakably a play.

57
00:04:30,463 --> 00:04:33,583
When training finishes, scroll down to generate.

58
00:04:33,863 --> 00:04:38,831
I type "ROMEO:" as the start, and keep temperature at zero point eight.

59
00:04:39,111 --> 00:04:45,039
Now low temperature, zero point two: the text gets calmer, and starts repeating itself.

60
00:04:45,319 --> 00:04:49,135
High, one point four: wilder words, more nonsense.

61
00:04:49,415 --> 00:04:53,255
Same model, same weights, only the sampling changes.

62
00:04:54,055 --> 00:04:56,887
First, how early the structure appears.

63
00:04:57,167 --> 00:05:04,607
Before the model gets the words right, it learns the layout: capital letters after line breaks, names followed by colons.

64
00:05:04,887 --> 00:05:07,935
Structure is cheap; meaning is expensive.

65
00:05:08,215 --> 00:05:12,703
Second, how much temperature changes the personality of the same model.

66
00:05:12,983 --> 00:05:18,071
People often blame the model for being boring or erratic when it is the sampling setting.

67
00:05:18,351 --> 00:05:20,223
Third, how small this is.

68
00:05:20,503 --> 00:05:26,407
Under a million parameters, no GPU, a minute or two of training for a first impression.

69
00:05:26,687 --> 00:05:31,343
Everything you need to understand the core mechanism fits in one short file.

70
00:05:32,143 --> 00:05:34,615
Let me be clear about what this model is not.

71
00:05:34,895 --> 00:05:37,103
It won't write coherent Shakespeare.

72
00:05:37,383 --> 00:05:43,551
At this size and with this little training it produces text that looks like a play but doesn't make sense.

73
00:05:43,831 --> 00:05:50,383
It only has a context window of up to a hundred and twenty-eight characters, so it can't remember anything earlier.

74
00:05:50,663 --> 00:05:54,743
It is character-level, which is slower to learn than word-level models.

75
00:05:55,023 --> 00:06:03,495
I haven't compared it against other architectures or tuned anything systematically, so don't read any particular loss number as a benchmark.

76
00:06:03,775 --> 00:06:10,183
Speed depends on your CPU; I measured between roughly four and six steps per second on mine across runs.

77
00:06:10,463 --> 00:06:18,191
And a language model trained on a single book will repeat that book's style and quirks, so if you upload text, expect it to echo it.

78
00:06:18,471 --> 00:06:21,495
And again: I built this with an AI assistant.

79
00:06:21,775 --> 00:06:25,567
I ran it and checked the figures, but read the code yourself.

80
00:06:26,367 --> 00:06:28,431
Here are the obvious next steps.

81
00:06:28,711 --> 00:06:34,303
Visualise the attention weights, so you can see which earlier characters each position looks at.

82
00:06:34,583 --> 00:06:36,455
That's on my list.

83
00:06:36,735 --> 00:06:39,783
Train on your own writing, using the upload option.

84
00:06:40,063 --> 00:06:42,127
Try a word-level tokenizer.

85
00:06:42,407 --> 00:06:47,063
Or scale layers and embedding size up and measure what changes in the loss.

86
00:06:47,343 --> 00:06:52,167
All the code is in the repo linked in the description, no API key needed.

87
00:06:52,447 --> 00:07:01,063
If you enjoyed watching a GPT go from gibberish to something play-shaped, follow for more AI and ML builds, and tell me what text you'd feed it.
